  "readmeFile": "nuget.md",
  "releaseNotes": "A query's dynamic rows cost less to build and to write as JSON. Each row is now a DbQueryRow, where it was an ExpandoObject. The rows of one result share their column names, so a row takes two allocations, where an ExpandoObject grew one column at a time. A row reads as before: as dynamic (row.name), or as IDictionary<string, object?> with case-sensitive names, and it raises PropertyChanged when a column changes. A name used twice still keeps its first column, and a JSON or XML column ({type{json{name}}}) is still parsed into ExpandoObjects. System.Text.Json writes a row byte for byte as it wrote the ExpandoObject, whatever the options, encoding each column name once per result, and reads one with JsonElement values. Code that tests a row's type (row is ExpandoObject) should test for DbQueryRow or IDictionary<string, object?> instead. In DBToRestAPI's benchmark, on one CPU, a response of 1,000 rows took 41% less CPU, and one of 100 rows 22% less; building 1,000 rows allocates 58% less memory.",
